\details{
The density uses the \code{conditioning_depth} stored by
\code{as_zplot()}. Recreate the zplot object to change the predictive target.

The line is the posterior mean of the density at each z value, and its
credible band (\code{plot_ci = TRUE}, or \code{y_lCI} and \code{y_uCI} of the
returned data) is formed by pointwise Harrell-Davis quantiles of the posterior
density draws at the probabilities \code{probs}, computed by
\code{\link[BayesTools:harrell_davis_quantile]{BayesTools::harrell_davis_quantile()}}. They estimate the same quantiles as the
empirical quantiles of the draws but smooth the kinks that the empirical
quantiles show along z. The bands remain pointwise, and, because every draw
contributes, they can differ visibly from the empirical quantiles when few
draws are used (\code{max_samples} below a few hundred). Near the edge of a
point mass in the draws (e.g., draws of models that fix the plotted quantity
in a model-averaged posterior) the band blends the point mass and the
remaining draws, and extreme probabilities (e.g., \code{probs = c(.0005, .9995)})
are not estimated more accurately than by the empirical quantiles; see
\code{\link[BayesTools:harrell_davis_quantile]{BayesTools::harrell_davis_quantile()}} for these caveats.

When \code{extrapolate = FALSE}, the density includes all bias adjustments
(PET/PEESE regression, selection weights) representing the fitted model.
When \code{extrapolate = TRUE}, bias adjustments are removed to show the
hypothetical distribution without publication bias. Multivariate models,
applicable integrated contexts, and best-rule selection models use a
normalized pre-selection Gaussian reference. Released univariate product
targets retain inverse-weight scaling
and their extrapolated curve need not integrate to one.
